Loring: Does anyone else have something they'd like to add to the agenda?

33
00:07:53.280 --> 00:08:02.809
Loring: Okay, then we will dive in. Starting off with enshrinement, are we okay with a non-PQ temporary solution?

34
00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:11.869
Loring: There was a robust conversation last week among some of the cryptographers,

35
00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:25.500
Loring: Does anyone have, either a summary of that conversation they could provide, or, some comments regarding the question itself?

36
00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:40.400
Gottfried Herold: Well, I mean, I'm not… I don't 100% recall all of the argument that we basically made last time, so this is a bit more on a more personal take. I mean, even to the question itself, I mean…

37
00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:52.870
Gottfried Herold: I think it roughly… it very, very strongly depends on what kind of temporary solution you have. I mean, you can have a solution where all of the non-PQ stuff happens essentially off-chain.

38
00:08:52.910 --> 00:09:08.579
Gottfried Herold: And, sort of, the protocol itself is not strongly aware of, sort of, one of it, or one where it's very, very strongly enshrined. So, in a way, it depends. I mean, you could have a solution where you say, hey, you have this kind of scheme.

39
00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:19.300
Gottfried Herold: And then you basically flip one switch, and then you change to maybe some kind of future PQ-safe scheme, and then everything will work out fine, and the required change is minimal.

40
00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:24.849
Gottfried Herold: This is very strongly in contrast if you sort of have to build the whole system up from scratch.

41
00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:37.370
Gottfried Herold: a new, with a potentially completely different design for a PQ-safe scheme. And so, I think this kind of question cannot really be decoupled from the question, what design you want at all.

42
00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:43.669
Gottfried Herold: That's… that's the unfortunate, well, take on this.

43
00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:51.089
Gottfried Herold: Because some schemes are very, very much, much more easy to upgrade to a PQ-safe solution than others.

44
00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:08.009
Gottfried Herold: So, I mean, for a more… on a more…

45
00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:23.610
Gottfried Herold: Well, well, a bit of a more… put it on the more action-level side. I mean, the question is more, okay, what kind of design do we even want, and sort of how bad is sort of optionality? Do we want to avoid it? Do we even want to use threshold encryption or not?

46
00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:48.040
Gottfried Herold: Like, I mean, if you sort of go for some Lucid-style design, which essentially does not strongly have all of this kind of threshold encryption in protocol, or if you do something else which does it, then it sort of very, very strongly depends, and I mean, at the end of the day, what we would need is a security analysis that basically states very precisely what sort of the requirements on the actual encryption scheme, or not encryption scheme, but on the cryptographic

47
00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:49.500
Gottfried Herold: Parts of it has.

48
00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:53.760
Gottfried Herold: Then you can evaluate, okay, what are the post-quantum alternatives?

49
00:10:54.820 --> 00:11:12.629
Gottfried Herold: And how likely do we believe such an alternative to exist at some point in the future? I mean, if it's just that, okay, we have some alternatives in principle, but they are maybe not efficient enough, then it's a very different picture than if we had no idea at all how to build such a thing in a post-quantum secure way.

50
00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:24.640
Gottfried Herold: That's… that's basically kind of what it boils down to, I think. So, I think this is sort of a question that should be asked after the design, not sort of in advance.

51
00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:29.370
Gottfried Herold: No, that's just my take.

52
00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:37.310
Loring: Yeah, as… Jannik, go ahead, I saw you took yourself on… off mute.

53
00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:49.879
Jannik Luhn: No, I just wanted to, like, from what I remember from last week, we did discuss that it might make sense to ask the broader Ethereum community first as well, because if…

54
00:11:50.230 --> 00:12:08.410
Jannik Luhn: they could say that having anything that's not PQ in the protocol is, like, not an option at all, then that already constrains us a lot, so that might already be valuable information to get even before we start designing anything.

55
00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:28.649
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, I mean, the thing is that designing anything sort of… I mean, usually when you design anything, you sort of design, hey, we have this kind of magic black box cryptography, and then you ask later what the, like, how to actually instantiate the black box, so it's kind of…

56
00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:35.460
Gottfried Herold: sort of the position. The PQ safety comes light in the design process anyway, but I agree with Jannik.

57
00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:36.700
Gottfried Herold: Always.

58
00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:52.610
Loring: as a non-cryptographer and as a non-protocol developer, it was a really interesting conversation, and, I would say my summary was

59
00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:56.189
Loring: There were kind of 3 questions which are…

60
00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:58.869
Loring: would help us answer this question.

61
00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:11.449
Loring: And Gottfried, I think you mentioned the last on my list, but the other two are, how much time and effort would it take Ethereum core developers to implement

62
00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:15.219
Loring: the non-PQ temporary solution.

63
00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:24.509
Loring: The second question is, how long will that non-PQ temporary solution remain effective?

64
00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:33.780
Loring: And there was a lot of interesting conversation there about how soon can we expect PQ,

65
00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:46.759
Loring: whether, the public Mempool would be a juicy, target for someone with that computing power. And if it was attacked,

66
00:13:47.010 --> 00:14:04.899
Loring: would an attack be detectable, and what would be the fallback, in case of attack? And I think those are all interesting questions which, we probably just scratched the surface on and did not reach any conclusions on. And then, the third

67
00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:23.689
Loring: question, which Gottfried mentioned, is how difficult would it be to transition from a non-PQ temporary solution to a PQ, more permanent solution? And I think there's both a technical question there, but also

68
00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:41.040
Loring: an experience and learning question there as well. Maybe some of the work done on the non-PQ temporary solution would be relevant and informative, for implementing the PQ more permanent solution.

69
00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:49.500
Loring: Feel free to correct me if I got anything wrong, but… For a layperson's summary,

70
00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:51.790
Loring: That's what I took away.

71
00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:53.620
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, that's perfectly correct.

72
00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:00.440
Gottfried Herold: But I mean, all of these questions depend on the scheme.

73
00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:05.969
Gottfried Herold: There are the answers to all of… most of these questions depend on the scheme, of course, or how you use

74
00:15:06.130 --> 00:15:07.829
Gottfried Herold: Sort of the encryption.

75
00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:24.979
Loring: Any additional comments regarding that before we move on to the next topic?

76
00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:37.639
Loring: Okay, next we'll, pick up on the conversation regarding the Validator whitelist.

77
00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:52.280
Loring: Jannik has prepared some additional, information, which begins to tackle the questions, how do we prove withholding and key selling, and can these proofs be automated?

78
00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:55.559
Loring: So I'll hand it over to Jannik.

79
00:15:56.750 --> 00:16:00.050
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, so,

80
00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:05.970
Jannik Luhn: Loring is showing the table here I created after the discussion we had in the previous call.

81
00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:15.770
Jannik Luhn: I try to understand them better for myself and organize it a little bit better for me, and maybe it's helpful for others too. I split it…

82
00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:28.840
Jannik Luhn: I call them now confidentiality and availability attacks, instead of, key selling and withholding attacks. Confidentiality, because it's more broad than just key selling,

83
00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:31.939
Jannik Luhn: I mean, the point is that the key is then not,

84
00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:50.419
Jannik Luhn: yeah, secret anymore. It could be the committee itself that's, that's using the key. They could sell the key as a whole, they could sell key shares, they could sell even individual decrypted transactions, and I think confidentiality is, yeah, covers that a little bit better. And then withholding, I think, is a fine name, but…

85
00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:52.720
Jannik Luhn: In… to keep it, kind of…

86
00:16:52.820 --> 00:17:00.879
Jannik Luhn: analogous to confidentiality, I called it availability attack, basically meaning that the key is not available at the time when it's supposed to.

87
00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:19.000
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, and to the two questions, how to detect it and what to do about it, there's two, rows in the bottom. I think the content we already discussed in the previous call, basically, availability is detected by the protocol itself via timeless committees, so we can do…

88
00:17:19.060 --> 00:17:24.899
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, slashing economic penalties, we can remove the,

89
00:17:25.099 --> 00:17:33.579
Jannik Luhn: the mechanism automatically, or the publisher automatically, if you want to. So that's relatively easy.

90
00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:42.479
Jannik Luhn: the confidentiality attack is in general, I think, yeah, undetectable. Maybe in some cases, depending on the protocol, there are…

91
00:17:42.590 --> 00:17:58.429
Jannik Luhn: like, things like snitching proofs, or, maybe you can slash them for… if you get ahold of someone's key share, but in general, these don't really work, or as circumventable by, using TEs, for example, or other mechanisms.

92
00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:10.300
Jannik Luhn: So, I think we just have to live with them, basically, and then mitigate them via, yeah, social accountability, reputation, and large setups.

93
00:18:10.340 --> 00:18:27.460
Jannik Luhn: But also, it's not that big of a deal, because the victims of confidentiality in this one-line affected parties there is basically only the user who explicitly chose, this particular key publisher, so it's bounded the, kind of.

94
00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:39.140
Jannik Luhn: the risk, whereas the availability attack is much, much larger. A set of people are affected, even people who did not choose this key publisher.

95
00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:48.499
Jannik Luhn: For instance, transactions that come afterwards, in a block, or arguably even the protocol as a whole, because, the…

96
00:18:49.030 --> 00:18:52.930
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, the chain gets spammed a little bit, because…

97
00:18:53.130 --> 00:19:02.799
Jannik Luhn: People want to make use of the optionality they get from running this availability tech, but they can only do this very inefficiently, so a lot of space is wasted by that.

98
00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:18.609
Jannik Luhn: But yeah, fortunately, since we can detect it automatically in the protocol, this is much easier to mitigate. So, yeah, nothing new there, just basically the summary of the, of what I took away from the conversation we had in the previous

99
00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:19.949
Jannik Luhn: previous time.

100
00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:31.379
Loring: And would it be correct to say that, kind of, the ultimate mitigation would be

101
00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:34.910
Loring: Exclusion from the whitelist.

102
00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:39.130
Loring: Thus preventing them from providing keys?

103
00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:58.199
Jannik Luhn: Yes, yeah. I mean, ideally, you prevent it in advance, that's kind of the idea of slashing, right? That you make it expensive so that it doesn't happen in the first place. If you have to exclude them after the fact, it's arguably already too late, depending on the,

104
00:19:58.380 --> 00:20:03.170
Jannik Luhn: the impact the attack had. But yeah,

105
00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:10.539
Jannik Luhn: That's kind of the idea of the whitelist, that they only select, key publishers that are,

106
00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:20.060
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, expected to behave correctly, and if they behaved incorrectly in the past, that's a good sign that they won't do that in the future and should be removed.

107
00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:37.030
Jannik Luhn: either manually, via the whitelist mechanism that the validators just say, remove them, like, they don't vote on them anymore, or, as I said, in that case of availability attacks, that might even be automated if,

108
00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:49.180
Jannik Luhn: yeah, I guess we would still need some sort of threshold there that a single, accidental non-reveal, or even maybe censorship by some committee doesn't lead to that, but…

109
00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:53.070
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, in theory, it could be automated.

110
00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:18.490
Jannik Luhn: Personally, I'm quite happy with that, state so far. The main question I have for the whitelist is, is it acceptable by the community to,

111
00:21:18.890 --> 00:21:29.070
Jannik Luhn: put this responsibility on the belly data set to vote on… to either vote directly on key publishers and select them, or to, select a committee that does that.

112
00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:38.530
Jannik Luhn: Both might be… Yeah, there's arguments against either of these, you know, options.

113
00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:49.950
Loring: Oh, wow.

114
00:21:54.560 --> 00:22:10.640
Loring: Does anyone have thoughts on… this table on the acceptability of the mitigation strategies, or… giving…

115
00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:13.760
Loring: That additional role to validators.

116
00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:24.530
Gottfried Herold: I mean, who else should have that role, right?

117
00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:34.099
Jannik Luhn: So it could be, in theory, also, ETH holders. We could do,

118
00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:40.949
Jannik Luhn: kind of a coin vote, but I think that's way worse, to be honest.

119
00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:43.600
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, yeah, it's much harder to sort of do…

120
00:22:43.930 --> 00:22:54.110
Gottfried Herold: do in practice. I mean, you need, in order to, sort of, for the deterrent to be meaningful, you need to be… it needs to be kind of somewhat hard to get into the whitelist.

121
00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:57.580
Gottfried Herold: So, yeah.

122
00:22:58.820 --> 00:23:07.269
Gottfried Herold: And at any rate, I mean, most of our… most of all ETHIN circulation is staked anyway, or not most, but a significant fraction is staked anyway.

123
00:23:08.270 --> 00:23:10.890
Jannik Luhn: So the difference is not… not as big.

124
00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:11.640
Gottfried Herold: Yeah.

125
00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:22.509
Jannik Luhn: I mean, briefly, I also thought about could we just let each, proposer make that choice?

126
00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:26.169
Jannik Luhn: But that's also very annoying, because, then…

127
00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:31.020
Jannik Luhn: You have, like, potentially very differing, sets of…

128
00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:45.930
Jannik Luhn: key publishers, and maybe if you choose the wrong one, it takes a long time for you to get accepted. And if you're a key publisher, you need to convince every individual Validator, basically, to accept you. So, I think that's also impractical, and I think Validator voting's the…

129
00:23:46.170 --> 00:23:48.020
Jannik Luhn: By far the most sensible choice.

130
00:23:49.990 --> 00:23:50.750
Loring: Oh, boy.

131
00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:15.630
Loring: Jannik, you said regarding confidentiality attacks that the…

132
00:24:17.830 --> 00:24:25.170
Loring: Damage is bounded because users can choose which of the whitelisted

133
00:24:25.330 --> 00:24:30.919
Loring: Key publishers they would like to use, and…

134
00:24:31.140 --> 00:24:44.460
Loring: by choosing a particular key publisher, they are kind of accepting the risks which come with that key publisher and their stated model and their track record.

135
00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:47.469
Loring: We know that a lot of the

136
00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:55.090
Loring: users do not do their own research and go more on vibes alone.

137
00:24:55.220 --> 00:25:01.529
Loring: Could you imagine a situation where a…

138
00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:11.090
Loring: Key publisher regularly engaged in confidentiality attacks, allowing them to frontrun or sandwich.

139
00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:18.460
Loring: But because they had sufficiently good marketing, or maybe,

140
00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:25.450
Loring: Connections to, popular, players.

141
00:25:26.170 --> 00:25:33.229
Loring: that… They would not, have that social accountability.

142
00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:40.919
Gottfried Herold: I mean, it's kind of what happened in what's usually called Encatification, right?

143
00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:58.039
Gottfried Herold: outside of the Ethereum world. Something… somebody used to be reputable, but I don't know, business models change, and then they basically drop out of their… of their game and sort of try to take some profit with them, as they basically drop out from being socially reputable.

144
00:26:02.300 --> 00:26:04.159
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, I mean, true, can happen.

145
00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:05.420
Gottfried Herold: Yeah.

146
00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:27.010
Gottfried Herold: I mean, my expectation would be for the choice of, sort of, provider, would be that wallets come basically pre-configured with a set of… with a default set. That would be the expectation they would see in reality. Probably wallet providers, maybe hardware wallets or whatever, would pro… might actually do such a service themselves.

147
00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:35.790
Gottfried Herold: And sort of hardwire their own provider into their sold… into the wallets they sell, into their products, in particular for hardware wallets.

148
00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:38.760
Gottfried Herold: It's kind of a reasonable business model, actually.

149
00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:45.210
Gottfried Herold: To sort of default or nudge people into this… into using their services this way.

150
00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:57.620
Jannik Luhn: you could make the same argument, Loring what you said about, private Mempools as well. They kind of…

151
00:26:58.210 --> 00:27:09.619
Jannik Luhn: their value propositions that you don't get Frontrun if you use them. They already achieved pretty big, I'm not sure if monopoly is the right term, but,

152
00:27:09.620 --> 00:27:11.459
Gottfried Herold: You know, legal police policies.

153
00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:18.330
Jannik Luhn: So they would be in a position to do that, to kind of, I don't know, front-run every 10th transaction, or…

154
00:27:18.560 --> 00:27:26.040
Jannik Luhn: front-run 10% of their, yeah, extract 10% of their front-running potential, and they won't do that.

155
00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:30.449
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, I mean, the question is, in some sense, very, very strongly tied to the… I think.

156
00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:47.339
Gottfried Herold: to the, moating or to the market design to sort of enter the whitelist. Because the question is, I mean, okay, if things go down, how easy is it for a newcomer to enter the market in case a legally of, sort of, bad, decryption services actually goes rogue?

157
00:27:47.990 --> 00:28:04.649
Gottfried Herold: And this is strongly tied to how the actual market or the voting mechanism for whitelist inclusion works. I mean, do you vote on each individual whitelist participant individually, or do you vote on sets of them? And there is a lot of questions, actually, for this design, and I think

158
00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:06.030
Gottfried Herold: It matters a lot.

159
00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:13.930
Jannik Luhn: Okay, interesting. I always thought the voting mechanism, like, the details don't matter too much, but…

160
00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:38.750
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, I mean, if you say you vote on a whole set of things… I mean, the problem is if you vote on… this is the set of whitelisted addresses, then the problem is that if you have, I don't know, 20 different candidates, you have 2 to the 20 different options, which means that a single mo… if you sort of go with a single minority, or a simple minority, then actually, basically you don't… you might not need a whole… a big fraction of total

161
00:28:38.750 --> 00:28:43.150
Gottfried Herold: Each stake to get your wish… your wish set in, for instance.

162
00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:44.960
Jannik Luhn: Cool.

163
00:28:44.960 --> 00:29:02.070
Gottfried Herold: So, yeah, you have to sort of… or you do some kind of ranked voting, you want… I mean, what's the threshold? Do you want… you don't… it's not clear you want the best of those to get in. I mean, how do you do it? You propose something, and if at least 50% of E sort of says, hey, yeah, this is a good… a good, sort of set, then yes, I get in.

164
00:29:02.410 --> 00:29:15.880
Gottfried Herold: But that also means that most… since most validators might not care about this, and I mean, a lot of these validators run actually in some automated stuff, I mean, I don't know, an at-home staker, maybe you get a future where at-home staking becomes much more…

165
00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:29.950
Gottfried Herold: present, let's say we do change issuance or whatever happens, the whole sort of staking, landscape might change in the future. You have a lot of home stakers, and I mean, your regular home staker does not want to care about, hey, there is this new, I don't know.

166
00:29:29.950 --> 00:29:37.360
Gottfried Herold: company which provides decryption services, hey, please vote for us, all of this needs to be done manually, they just want their hardware to run, and…

167
00:29:37.370 --> 00:29:51.929
Gottfried Herold: And not deal with it manually. So it's kind of hard to get people to vote at all. Not so much to vote pro or con, it's hard to sort of get people to vote at all. So how do you actually do your set quora for this? Do you not do… there's a lot of questions, actually, here.

168
00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:55.549
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, so maybe I should, try to…

169
00:29:57.240 --> 00:30:05.780
Jannik Luhn: write this down a little bit, to the options we have there. Intuitively, I would say, yes, we do want to have delegation to avoid this, so that home stickers don't have to make that choice.

170
00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:25.109
Jannik Luhn: And for the voting mechanism itself, I would have said, each vote is independent, and there can be an infinite number of publishers, so… and as long as one key publisher, or each one gets more than 50%, they get accepted. Because I don't really see,

171
00:30:25.110 --> 00:30:32.589
Gottfried Herold: 50% of… okay, so you just have yes votes or no vote, and you want… you need a 50% of all. You don't have… you don't have against votes.

172
00:30:32.900 --> 00:30:34.570
Gottfried Herold: And not war room.

173
00:30:34.570 --> 00:30:38.800
Jannik Luhn: No, like, no vote is against… Okay. Always.

174
00:30:39.370 --> 00:30:40.630
Jannik Luhn: To keep it simple.

175
00:30:40.630 --> 00:30:45.839
Gottfried Herold: So, but this means you cannot, sort of, remove people from the whitelist, right?

176
00:30:46.510 --> 00:30:50.190
Jannik Luhn: You can buy… I mean, it has to be, kind of,

177
00:30:51.780 --> 00:31:02.699
Jannik Luhn: I mean, the Validator set potentially changes, so it always has to be updated anyways, right? So, kind of, the past N validators, are the set of voters.

178
00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:09.460
Jannik Luhn: And in that, they… maybe they're more with every blog that they publish.

179
00:31:11.200 --> 00:31:15.910
Gottfried Herold: Well, so you mean you re-vote, so you basically keep voting?

180
00:31:15.910 --> 00:31:16.410
Jannik Luhn: Yeah.

181
00:31:16.410 --> 00:31:20.460
Gottfried Herold: Not just for changes. Now, that's a lot of bandwidth, potentially, but okay.

182
00:31:20.630 --> 00:31:21.550
Jannik Luhn: That's true.

183
00:31:22.300 --> 00:31:27.130
Jannik Luhn: Okay, yeah, I see that's a good point. I should, think more about this, and…

184
00:31:28.550 --> 00:31:31.400
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, write something down for the next call.

185
00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:35.080
Loring: Couple of related questions.

186
00:31:35.920 --> 00:31:48.709
Loring: Jannik, did you have… A ideal number of key publishers, or a maximum number of key publishers in mind?

187
00:31:50.930 --> 00:32:10.180
Jannik Luhn: So, I don't think there should be a maximum number, I don't see why there should be, as long as… I mean, the only, like, from the protocol perspective, the only thing that matters is, that the key publishers don't engage in these availability attacks, and any, key publisher that doesn't do that can be accepted.

188
00:32:11.560 --> 00:32:14.020
Jannik Luhn: I don't see a reason why that should be limited.

189
00:32:15.210 --> 00:32:19.310
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, from a pers… like…

190
00:32:19.420 --> 00:32:25.260
Jannik Luhn: Competition is always good, so the more the merrier. How many it's going to be in practice.

191
00:32:25.530 --> 00:32:26.539
Jannik Luhn: I don't know.

192
00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:30.299
Jannik Luhn: Probably not too many, maybe 5.

193
00:32:35.930 --> 00:32:37.530
Loring: and would…

194
00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:51.030
Loring: I'm not sure exactly how to phrase this, but the gist of it is, should the validators have…

195
00:32:51.480 --> 00:32:57.360
Loring: preferences about… Who should…

196
00:32:57.460 --> 00:33:03.620
Loring: Be a key provider, or what type of key provider should be accepted above and beyond

197
00:33:03.820 --> 00:33:12.310
Loring: just a commitment not to engage in availability or withholding of tax. For example,

198
00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:15.669
Loring: Should there be a preference for…

199
00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:25.279
Loring: Key providers which provide the service as a public good, as opposed to, doing it on a commercial basis.

200
00:33:25.710 --> 00:33:32.159
Loring: Should there be a preference against key provider or key publishers?

201
00:33:32.500 --> 00:33:33.900
Loring: Which are…

202
00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:45.769
Loring: also run by a wallet which has a tight coupling? Or do you see that, validators should just kind of certify

203
00:33:46.440 --> 00:33:51.430
Loring: They don't engage in availability attacks, that's good enough, we don't think about anything else.

204
00:33:53.060 --> 00:34:01.860
Jannik Luhn: I would say the latter, to make it as easy as possible for them, and that's already difficult enough, I think, to predict in the first place.

205
00:34:02.180 --> 00:34:03.290
Jannik Luhn: In advance.

206
00:34:08.320 --> 00:34:17.160
Gottfried Herold: I mean, from a protocol perspective, the thing that we want to defend the protocol against is essentially someone basically just making a new

207
00:34:17.350 --> 00:34:30.260
Gottfried Herold: basically decryption service for each individual transaction, and then withholding or not, basically doing an optionality attack. That's basically the attack that you want to defend against. So the barriers can and should be actually very low.

208
00:34:34.420 --> 00:34:35.620
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, I agree.

209
00:34:41.639 --> 00:34:47.859
Gottfried Herold: And then selection of the actual, sort of, service providers, you know, done by economic… Means.

210
00:34:50.420 --> 00:34:52.699
Jannik Luhn: I mean, there could even be, an…

211
00:34:53.110 --> 00:35:00.489
Jannik Luhn: like I mentioned, slashing is possible for, for recording.

212
00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:14.779
Jannik Luhn: And there could be an optional stake, and new key publishers might have to put a lot of stake up there so that… to convince validators, which can then be reduced over time once they've gained trust by the validator set.

213
00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:18.210
Jannik Luhn: So that might be another parameter to take into account.

214
00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:27.639
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, slashing for withholding is a bit problematic, because then, basically some kind of blackout, or some internet outage, or this is sort of…

215
00:35:28.080 --> 00:35:30.919
Gottfried Herold: Sort of puts a high operational risk.

216
00:35:33.150 --> 00:35:36.800
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, but I mean, these are supposed to be professional, it's not like,

217
00:35:36.800 --> 00:35:37.300
Gottfried Herold: That's.

218
00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:39.359
Jannik Luhn: Not the equivalent of a homestaker.

219
00:35:39.620 --> 00:35:40.660
Gottfried Herold: True, true.

220
00:35:50.130 --> 00:35:52.959
Loring: Any other questions or comments?

221
00:35:53.680 --> 00:35:54.960
Loring: on this topic.

